Chapter: BrowserKeysTest
  cites Browser chapter BrowserEvent
  cites UI chapter Event
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Proof that the browser decodes its Ctrl and Alt chords now that its event
 wrapper stamps `poll-mods` onto the `EvKeyDown` and its mod constants match
 UI Event (shift 1, ctrl 2, alt 4). Each `probe` feeds `map-key-event` a
 keycode and a modifier mask and prints the action it maps to. A letter
 arrives lowercase (poll-key is case-sensitive), so `map-ctrl-key` folds it
 through `key-upper` before matching -- the reason `t` (116) still reaches
 New Tab. The chords, in order:

   Ctrl+t -> New Tab (1), Ctrl+w -> Close Tab (2), Ctrl+l -> Focus Address
   (3), Ctrl+r -> Reload (4), Alt+Left -> Back (5), Alt+Right -> Forward
   (6), Ctrl+Shift+Tab -> Prev Tab (7), Ctrl+Tab -> Next Tab (8).

Section: Test

  act-code : BrowserAction -> Integer
  act-code (a) =
    when a
      is ActionNewTab -> 1
      is ActionCloseTab -> 2
      is ActionFocusAddress -> 3
      is ActionReload -> 4
      is ActionBack -> 5
      is ActionForward -> 6
      is ActionPrevTab -> 7
      is ActionNextTab -> 8
      is otherwise -> 0

  probe : Integer, Integer -> [Console] Nothing
  probe (k) (mods) = act
    print-line-uni (integer-to-text (act-code (map-key-event (event-key-down k mods 0))))
  end

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    probe 116 2
    probe 119 2
    probe 108 2
    probe 114 2
    probe 37 4
    probe 39 4
    probe 9 3
    probe 9 2
  end
